## LEVEL I STATUS

`STILL OPEN`

The run proves an exact finite-amplitude strengthening of Sections 142–145 and 153.
It does not prove the Target A Schur inequality.

## 1. Starting endpoint

The required closure was and remains

\[
\boxed{\dim\ker S\ge\Delta_\Lambda-d_{\rm int}},
\]

equivalently

\[
\boxed{
\operatorname{rank}S
\le d_{\rm int}+|C^\partial|-\Delta_\Lambda.
}
\]

The activation route hoped to turn the independent rows created by the canonical
monotone activation sequence into a compensating loss of boundary-response rank.
Section 153 ruled out positive KKT load on those rows but left their unweighted
active/critical-cone geometry open.

The audited implication from this run is diagnostic rather than closing:

```text
full created-pair forest
-> exact independent affine release coordinates
-> terminal activation rows are not a locking/cage mechanism
-> any surviving activation-to-Schur theorem needs additional global transport data
```

## 2. Strongest genuinely new audited theorem

### Theorem

Let

\[
A_0,A_1,\ldots,A_\ell
\]

be a monotone activation sequence from Theorem 142.1, and let \(F\) contain every
unit or diameter pair created by the sequence. Then:

1. \(F\) is a forest;
2. each component of \(F\) contains at most one vertex incident to an active pair
   of \(A_0\);
3. there is \(\varepsilon>0\) and an affine embedding
   \[
   \Psi:[0,\varepsilon)^F\to(\mathbb R^2)^N
   \]
   through the terminal configuration such that every image point is a triangle-free
   global minimizer of diameter \(D\);
4. if \(e\) was created as a unit contact, its length is exactly
   \(1+\theta_e\), and if it was created as a diameter pair, its length is exactly
   \(D(1-\theta_e)\);
5. all initial active pairs remain exact, while any prescribed subset of created
   pairs can be released independently; and
6. when all coordinates are positive, the strict-rattler set is exactly the
   strict-rattler set of \(A_0\).

### Scope

The theorem concerns the actual global-minimizer sequence of Theorem 142.1. It is
finite and exact, preserves the global minimum diameter, and treats unit and diameter
activations simultaneously. It is local in parameter size: no uniform lower bound on
\(\varepsilon\) is claimed. It concerns the source representative only and does not
coordinate-identify that representative with the independent smaller optimum or the
Schur scaffold.

### Proof

Let \(E_0\) be the initial active-pair set and write the pair created at step \(i\)
as \(e_i=r_iq_i\), where \(r_i\) is the moved point. Immediately before that step,
\(r_i\) is strict, so it is isolated in

\[
E_0\cup\{e_0,\ldots,e_{i-1}\}.
\]

Every created edge therefore adds an isolated endpoint. This cannot form a cycle.
It also cannot merge two existing created-edge components, and the isolated endpoint
is not incident to \(E_0\). Induction proves that the full created-pair graph \(F\)
is a forest and that each component contains at most one initial-active vertex.

Use that vertex as the component root when it exists, choose any root otherwise,
and orient the forest away from its roots. At the terminal coordinates \(p_v\), for
an oriented edge \(e=uv\) put

\[
d_e=p_v-p_u,
\qquad
\sigma_e=
\begin{cases}
+1,&e\text{ is a unit contact},\\
-1,&e\text{ is a diameter pair}.
\end{cases}
\]

Fix all roots and all vertices outside \(F\). For a vertex \(v\) in a rooted
component define

\[
w_v(\theta)=\sum_{e\in P(v)}\sigma_e\theta_e d_e,
\qquad
p_v(\theta)=p_v+w_v(\theta),
\]

where \(P(v)\) is its root path. If \(e=uv\), the two path sums differ only in
the term for \(e\), so

\[
p_v(\theta)-p_u(\theta)
=(1+\sigma_e\theta_e)d_e.
\]

This gives the asserted exact lengths. Every vertex incident to \(E_0\) is a fixed
root or lies outside \(F\), so every initial active pair remains exact. All other
terminally inactive pairs have distances strictly between \(1\) and \(D\). Finiteness
and continuity give one \(\varepsilon>0\) for which those strict inequalities persist;
take it also smaller than \(D-1\) and \(1-1/D\).

The resulting configurations are unit-separated and have diameter at most \(D\).
An initial diameter pair is fixed, so their diameter is exactly \(D=D(N)\); hence
all are global minimizers. Their contact graphs are subgraphs of the terminal
triangle-free contact graph, so they remain triangle-free. Finally, each created
edge length recovers its own coordinate, proving injectivity. Positive coordinates
release exactly the selected pairs. With every coordinate positive, the active set is
exactly \(E_0\), which restores precisely the initial strict-rattler set. \(\square\)

### Dependencies

- `docs/targets/target-a/03-source-response.md`, Theorem 142.1;
- `docs/targets/target-a/07-activation-stress-invariance.md`, Section 153 only for
  comparison, not for the proof.

### Why it is new

Section 144 records only selected first unit-activation edges and proves that this
subgraph is a forest. The new theorem treats **all** created pairs, including diameter
activations, proves the one-initial-root property, and integrates their signed edge
directions into an exact independent affine family. Section 153 proves only universal
zero KKT multipliers; it does not construct simultaneous finite deactivations or the
Boolean family of active-set patterns.

## 3. Exact target implication

The numerical Schur endpoint is unchanged. What changes is the permitted mechanism:
the independent activation rows cannot be used as an unweighted terminal cage. They
are coordinate facets of a realizable global-optimum orthant, and even a single row
can be released while every other created and initial row stays exact.

This does not prove that all uses of activation data fail. It proves that any useful
activation theorem must use information beyond terminal locking, such as finite
collision walls outside the local orthant, a relation between different optimum
cardinalities, or an exact source-to-scaffold transport.

## 4. Exact remaining lemma

The general endpoint remains the canonical Schur inequality. In the aligned
very-super-boundary subbranch of Section 155, the most concrete remaining theorem is:

> **Two-shadow compatibility lemma.** Under (155.4), choose or exchange the two
> near-saturating matchings from the smaller optimum and the inner scaffold into the
> same source so that their common source structure produces either
> \(\dim\ker S\ge\Delta_\Lambda-d_{\rm int}\) or an \(N\)-point unit-separated
> configuration of diameter strictly below \(D\).

The activation orthant alone supplies neither alternative.

## 5. Audit classification of other claims

| Claim | Classification | Reason |
| --- | --- | --- |
| The full created-pair graph is a forest | proved | Each new edge has the currently strict moved point as an isolated endpoint. |
| Every subset of created pairs is independently releasable | proved | The signed root-path affine coordinates give exact edge lengths. |
| The orthant yields Schur nullity | rejected | No source-to-independent-scaffold map is constructed. |
| The orthant yields a smaller-diameter competitor | rejected | An initial diameter pair is intentionally fixed. |
| Section 153 already contains the orthant | false/already distinguished | Section 153 concerns multipliers, not finite-amplitude feasibility. |
| A free Kuperberg support rate is canonical | rejected for promotion here | The active theorem stack contains no such promoted theorem. |

## 6. Regression tests

| Regression | Passed? | Exact reason |
| --- | --- | --- |
| long rooted chain | yes | The forest may be arbitrarily long and \(\varepsilon\) may shrink without a uniform bound. |
| two-leaf locked first-transition cage | yes | The activation theorem creates exactly one active pair at a moved strict point; the locked transition's simultaneous cage constraints are outside the hypothesis. |
| Section 152 activation/monomer regression | yes | No local activation-to-Schur implication is claimed. |
| Section 153 zero KKT load | yes | The exact release orthant is compatible with universal zero multipliers. |
| strained monomer / regular shell | yes | No extra point, support-radius estimate, or density gap is claimed. |
| overlapping circuits / full-rank planar response | yes | No circuit independence or planarity-to-nullity inference is used. |
| source/scaffold coordinate independence | yes | The theorem explicitly remains on the source side. |
| exact fixed-hull capacity | yes | No Hall deficiency above the hull slack is asserted. |

## 7. Failed routes and no-go results

1. **Positive KKT compensation:** already dead by Section 153; the present theorem
   gives the corresponding finite-amplitude geometry.
2. **Terminal critical-cone locking:** rejected. The entire signed release orthant is
   realized by triangle-free global minimizers.
3. **Independent-row count directly forces response nullity:** still unsupported.
   The theorem supplies no map from source coordinates to the strict gammoid.

The no-go is mechanism-specific. It does not rule out global transport through the
finite path or through the two source shadows of Section 155.

## 8. Literature imported

No external result enters the activation theorem. During the repository audit, the
sole external input of Section 155 was checked at theorem level:

### Daniel A. Klain, “An Error Estimate for the Isoperimetric Deficit”

- Link: [author PDF](https://faculty.uml.edu/dklain/Klain-Isoperimetric.pdf)
- Exact point: Section 4, equation (6).
- Hypotheses: compact convex planar set with perimeter \(P\), area \(A\),
  circumradius \(R\), and inradius \(r\).
- Conclusion: \(P^2-4\pi A\ge\pi^2(R-r)^2\).
- Applicability: exact for both convex bodies \(H\) and \(L\) in Section 155.
- Point of use: converts their canonical \(O(D)\) isoperimetric deficits into
  \(R-r=O(\sqrt D)\).

The citation and the missing generic-phase noncoincidence hypothesis are corrected in
the canonical Section 154–155 module as part of this audit.

